# Secrets. The Web3Forms access key and anything else sensitive lives in the
# Cloudflare Pages dashboard or a local .env — never in the repo.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Dependencies. Restored with `npm install`, so committing them just bloats
# the repo and breaks on a different OS.
node_modules/

# Screenshot originals. The pipeline (Section 7) captures full-size PNGs and
# converts them to optimised webp. Only the webp is worth versioning; the
# originals are large, regenerable, and would dominate the repo's history.
assets/work/**/*.png
assets/work/**/*.jpeg
assets/work/**/*.jpg
.shots-cache/

# Ben's about-page photo, straight off the phone. Ben's own copy is 11.7MB and
# lives outside the repo; convert-photo.js turns it into the webp variants
# actually committed under assets/brand/, the same rule the screenshot
# originals above already follow.
ben-laptop.jpg

# Ben's hand-made device mockups (a-nevitt-mockup-nobg.png, ajc-mockup-nobg.png,
# julie-miller-mockup-nobg.png, and the unused variants alongside them). UNLIKE
# the screenshot originals and the backdrop stills above, these are NOT
# regenerable by any script: they are one-off design-tool exports, several
# megabytes each, and if one is deleted from the repo it is gone for good.
# Ben keeps the source files himself; tools/convert-photo.js --mockup reads
# them once and writes the committed webp copies under assets/work/<slug>/.
*-mockup-nobg.png
*-mockup2-nobg.png
*-mockup-2-nobg*.png

# Backdrop stills and clips. `npm run backdrop` writes ~11MB of print-resolution
# PNGs for use behind documents; `npm run backdrop:video` writes ~60MB of MP4s
# for social posts. Same reasoning as the screenshots above: large, exactly
# regenerable from dot-field.js, and nothing on the site links to them, so
# versioning them would only bloat the history and publish them by accident.
assets/backdrop/

# Claude Code's per-machine approvals. settings.json IS committed (it's the
# shared permission policy); settings.local.json holds this machine's
# "don't ask again" answers and is personal.
.claude/settings.local.json

# OS and editor noise.
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
.DS_Store
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# The published site. tools/build.js writes dist/ from nothing on every run:
# the pages, the minified stylesheets and scripts, and the images they name.
# Every byte of it is regenerable with `npm run build`.
#
# IT IS COMMITTED ANYWAY, and the reason is the deploy. Cloudflare runs
# `npx wrangler deploy` on a push to main and nothing else, so whatever dist/
# holds at that moment is what goes live. Add `npm run build` to the Workers
# Build settings in the Cloudflare dashboard and this becomes a real ignore
# rule: uncomment the line below and `git rm -r --cached dist`.
#
# dist/
